{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LR6u8qaNQ4mFGefX57sHh5","type":"tool_result","content":"39\t## R1 · 2026-07-17 · C1 · The `onInstalled` → options-tab mechanism (carried forward)\n40\t\n41\t**The finding, paid for in real debugging.** `chrome.runtime.openOptionsPage()`, called\n42\tsynchronously from the `onInstalled` listener at cold `--load-extension` launch, resolves with no\n43\t`chrome.runtime.lastError` but **creates no tab**. The same call demonstrably works in the same\n44\tharness when triggered later by a user-gesture click. Whether this is a real Chrome quirk or a\n45\tPlaywright-harness artifact was never resolved — and does not need to be, because the same action\n46\twins under both readings.\n47\t\n48\t**Ruling: build the resilient mechanism. Do NOT accept a documented e2e-only limitation.**\n49\t\n50\tAn accepted limitation is refused precisely because it would exempt the category's foundation card\n51\tfrom the category's own proof gate (C10, already shipped, exists to be that gate).\n52\t\n53\t**Mechanism, in preference order:**\n54\t\n55\t1. **Preferred:** in the `onInstalled` listener, use\n56\t   `chrome.tabs.create({ url: chrome.runtime.getURL('options.html') })` rather than\n57\t   `chrome.runtime.openOptionsPage()`. `chrome.tabs.create` requires **no** permission (the `tabs`\n58\t   permission governs reading tab URLs/titles, which this does not do), and on a fresh install\n59\t   there is by definition no existing options tab, so `openOptionsPage()`'s only real advantage —\n60\t   reusing an open options tab — is moot here.\n61\t2. **If that also yields no tab at cold launch:** add a **bounded** retry — a small fixed number of\n62\t   attempts, never an unbounded or repeating loop.\n63\t3. **If the tab still cannot be produced** after 1 and 2: return `NEEDS_DIRECTION` with the\n64\t   evidence. Do **not** silently downgrade to an accepted limitation.\n65\t\n66\t**Do NOT use `chrome.tabs.query` to detect whether the tab appeared** — querying by URL requires\n67\tthe `tabs` permission, and a new manifest permission is an **owner-only** escalation\n68\t(`new-manifest-permission`; ROADMAP §6 E6). No new permission may be added under this ruling.\n69\t\n70\t**The scope fence is unchanged and still binds** (this is a mechanism swap, not a fence cut):\n71\tfires **only** on `reason === 'install'`, never on `'update'`; opens **exactly one tab, once** — no\n72\tre-prompting loop; skipped entirely when the build bakes an env key; **no new manifest permission**.\n73\tC1's \"deliberately minimal\" framing forbids adding _features_; it does not forbid making the one\n74\tspecified behavior actually work.\n75\t\n76\t**Keep the decision logic in the domain and the `chrome.*` calls in `sw.ts`**\n77\t(`rule-domain-purity`): the swap above is confined to the `sw.ts` side.\n78\t\n79\t---\n80\t\n81\t## R2 · 2026-07-17 · campaign-wide · Standing constraints (carried forward)\n82\t\n83\tInherited rulings, restated because every executor brief must carry them:\n84\t\n85\t1. **Regular merge commits only — squash-merge is prohibited** (owner, 2026-07-16). A merged card\n86\t   PR's merge commit has exactly 2 parents.\n87\t2. **Evidence is written, not media** (owner, 2026-07-16). Every PR body carries a **\"Testing\n88\t   performed\"** section — suites run, test counts, e2e scenarios exercised, gates passed. Do not\n89\t   capture screenshots or video.\n90\t3. **The Shaman authors How** (owner, 2026-07-16). Specs and plans are pre-written and committed on\n91\t   master. Executors run them; any plan-vs-reality mismatch beyond trivial mechanical drift is\n92\t   `NEEDS_DIRECTION`, not a unilateral redesign.\n93\t4. **`GEMINI_API_KEY` in the builder's shell bakes an env-key build that silently disables\n94\t   onboarding.** Use `bun run build:chrome:e2e` (which clears it) for every e2e build — this is\n95\t   C10's shipped guard and it supersedes any plan text still saying\n96\t   `env -u GEMINI_API_KEY bun run build:chrome`.\n97\t5. **Playwright spec filters use exact spec names**, never bare substrings.\n98\t6. **`docs/index.html` on master IS the deployed landing page** — treat any change to it as a\n99\t   production release. None of the cards in this campaign's sequence should need to touch it.\n100\t7. Standing constraints from ROADMAP §3 bind unchanged: S1 key isolation · S4 sanitize model output\n101\t   (including streamed/partial) · no background LLM calls · design tokens only · ports architecture\n102\t   with a dependency-free domain.\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## R3 · 2026-07-17 · C1 · The `onboarding.spec.ts` regression R1's fix exposes (carried forward)\n107\t\n108\tR1's `chrome.tabs.create` swap leaves a genuine `options.html` tab open from install. The no-key\n109\tcard's \"Open Settings\" CTA calls `chrome.runtime.openOptionsPage()`, whose real Chrome behavior is\n110\tto **focus/reuse an already-open same-URL tab** rather than create a new one. So\n111\t`onboarding.spec.ts`'s third test waits for a `'page'` event that never fires, and times out\n112\tdeterministically.\n113\t\n114\t**Ruling: update the test. Focusing an existing options tab IS the correct outcome. Do NOT build a\n115\tseparate tab-creation mechanism for the CTA.**\n116\t\n117\t- Focus-and-reuse is `openOptionsPage()`'s documented Chrome semantics, and it is the better\n118\t  product behavior: one settings tab, not two.\n119\t- **Duplicate settings tabs are actively harmful in _this_ product.** A16 shipped the sticky save\n120\t  bar and its \"Unsaved changes\" cue specifically because silent settings data loss is a real,\n121\t  already-shipped-against failure mode here. Two settings tabs means edit in one, save the other,\n122\t  lose the work.\n123\t- Giving the CTA its own tab-creation mechanism would mutate the **shared** router `openOptions`\n124\t  callback, changing behavior for every caller — a far wider blast radius than C1's fence.\n125\t- **No product promise changes**, so this is not an owner escalation: the CTA still takes the user\n126\t  to the options page. Nothing promises \"in a _new_ tab\".\n127\t\n128\t**MANDATORY GUARD — the replacement assertion MUST stay falsifiable.** The old\n129\t`waitForEvent('page')` had real power: it failed if the CTA did nothing. Do **NOT** replace it with\n130\t\"an `options.html` tab exists\" — that is now **tautological** (install already opened one), so it\n131\twould pass even if the button were completely broken. Silently gutting a live regression test while\n132\tappearing to fix it is the one outcome this ruling forbids.\n133\t\n134\tSatisfy it in this preference order:\n135\t\n136\t1. **Assert the transition the click causes:** immediately before the click the `options.html` tab\n137\t   is NOT the active tab; after the click it IS the active/focused tab. Additionally assert\n138\t   **exactly one** `options.html` tab exists afterwards (proves reuse, not duplication).\n139\t2. **If tab activation cannot be observed reliably** in this `--headless=new` harness: in the test's\n140\t   arrange step explicitly close the install-created options tab, then keep the original\n141\t   `waitForEvent('page')` assertion unchanged (proving the create path still works).\n142\t3. **Whichever you use, PROVE falsifiability:** temporarily break the CTA, show the test RED,\n143\t   restore it, show it GREEN. Record both runs in the report. A test that cannot be demonstrated to\n144\t   fail is not evidence.\n145\t\n146\t**Scope under this ruling:** `packages/extension-chrome/e2e/onboarding.spec.ts` is **IN** scope for\n147\tC1. The router's `openOptions` callback and R1's `sw.ts` swap are **NOT** to be changed.\n148\t\n149\t**Any plan text claiming \"no existing test's assertions change\" is SUPERSEDED** — it rested on a\n150\tmechanism (`openOptionsPage()` at install) that never actually worked, per R1. Say so plainly in the\n151\tPR's \"Testing performed\" section rather than quietly editing the test.\n152\t\n153\t---"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01B9iSw7CJVDP18fuoGDhi4c","session_id":"1582a562-7cd7-4915-90db-f6e0a641a53f","uuid":"4ab5b700-da47-4d29-a51d-25f347f39071","timestamp":"2026-07-24T00:25:44.660Z","subagent_type":"tribe:skinner","task_description":"Skinner: audit C1 done-ness"}
